\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The hover-limousine banked away from the pad and found an airspace corridor that took Chanta directly over Barangaroo\'97no waiting, no traffic, the kind of direct flight reserved for CEOs, misadventure capitalists, and Airborne lifestyle emergencies. He rifled through the minibar, sneering at expensive whiskies and brandies, bourbons and vodkas. All too refined for his cheap tastes. Still, wherever this journey was taking him, he needed ingested courage. Hating Tasmanian scotch more than any other brown liquid, he figured he\'92d hate the expensive stuff less\'97it might even taste coarse enough to almost enjoy. He pulled the stopper out with his teeth, spat it away, and drank a large draft straight from the bottle. It was smoother than a weak cup of tea, but not so bad it stopped him taking a second draft. As the limousine's relatively silent fans propelled him farther from safe ground, he swilled a third.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Chanta's usually dim curiosity was piqued when the hover-limo veered up from the pads over the railway and public concourse\'97only a floor or two above the roiling Layer today\'97and circled around with majestic ease to a private pad at the rear of Starborne One. The pad was black and dull as a rubber tyre, and huge\'97much larger than the limousine required. Surrounding it, a wide band of flower beds bloomed in colours both bright and pastel which, if Chanta had any knowledge of flowers, he would have recognised as roses.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Starborne One was a stately, ageing, yet immaculately maintained residential address constructed for one purpose: to provide unparalleled views from which the obscenely balance-sheet blessed could look down on lesser Airborne. That taller towers had been erected in the generations since its cathedral-sized apartments were sold off the plan was of no consequence to its mouldy old-money residents; they had simply taken to looking down in a metaphorical sense, knowing they could pull the social rug out from under newer money with the same calm efficiency they would bring to firing a personal assistant who had started using first names uninvited.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The property developers (a.k.a vertical kleptocrats)
\f2\fs24  
\f1\fs22 who conceived Starborne One had been marketing to a caste that did not exist until the building invented it. They were not merely building ever-taller residential towers that might one day require early-warning satellite collision systems; they were crowning the Ulyssian caste system with another tier, albeit one they were legally obliged to note, in the fine print of every prospectus, was not a real classification recognised in law by any organ of the Airborne Meritocracy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Starborne One had been designed in reverent obedience to the principle that enough was vulgar, too much was timid, and obscene was merely the first respectable offer. Ceilings rose to such heights that the larger residences developed their own weather patterns; an excessively long shower in the primary suite could cause fine snow to fall from the air-conditioning vents of the staff quarters. It was not uncommon for children to fly drones in the family room up to mezzanine altitudes where they crashed, vanished, and were eventually written off as part of the entertainment budget.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The complex housed its own day spa, a small hospital capable of the most elaborate cosmetic surgery, a cinema showing only X-rated art-house films and spaghetti westerns rated G, a casino with the city\'92s only cock-fighting ring, and a dungeon to which residents could consign domestic staff upon the formulation of a reasonably atrocious whim.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta braced for the bone-jarring landing, but the limousine landed as softly as a sigh falling asleep. When its port passenger door opened, Chanta swiped the scotch bottle and alighted.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 He located a break in the flower beds and followed the black path between them toward the main tower. Chanta barely remembered Halo\'92s silly verses, but \'93to the right the path goes\'94 stuck in his head, so he took a right fork. The garden was much larger than he\'92d guessed. He gazed about\'97roses, pathways, the kind of thing rich people wasted space on. The garden should have felt like solid ground, but his head was too light from breathing flower perfume and clean air to appreciate the illusion. The path merged with a promenade that clung to the building and curved out of sight to the left. The tower drew Chanta\'92s eyes to where it pressed against the impossibly blue sky. He lost all sense of perspective as he gazed, his chin jutting forward as if pulling the rest of his body along with it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That\'92s when Chanta tripped. He might not have fallen so hard had he not tried to save the whisky bottle. The expensive grog was saved, but Chanta bit his lip in the fall. Tending a finger to seeping blood, he looked back for what had tripped him. A thick black irrigation hose had been ripped from the garden beds and ran along the side of the path for some metres before it snaked across to the other side and continued onwards out of sight. Farther on, a naked, old, greyish man stood by the blank wall of the tower, posing as if taking a shower. A sparkle of water trickled over him from the end of the repurposed irrigation hose taped to the wall. Beyond the shower scene, advertising hoardings leaned against the building\'97a makeshift home Chanta was sure would be dismantled by security consultants before the day was out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While wringing out his clothes, the naked scrubber side-eyed Chanta passing by on the other side of a garden bed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the promenade, the polished black wall of the building confronted Chanta like a cliff face\'97a cliff face with a three-metre stylised rose etched and carved into it. As Chanta approached, the rose shifted: from near-invisible carving to lurid fluorescent painting to coppery wire-frame to something three-dimensional and alive. He squinted into the depths of the wall\'97was it smoky glass? Black opal? When sunlight struck it, the rose seemed to float in front of the surface, so real Chanta expected to feel velvet petals. He reached out. His fingers met nothing. Empty air. He recoiled with shock.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The jolt jogged his memory. He thought of Halo\'92s message and fumbled into his coat pocket, retrieved his tablet, and swiped and tapped until the card was on his screen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f3\i\fs24 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To the right the path goes
\f1\i0\fs22  \'97 Chanta nodded. Been there, done that.  \

\f3\i\fs24 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Don\'92t trip on the hose
\f1\i0\fs22  \'97 Chanta sucked on his damaged lip.  \

\f3\i\fs24 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Have no fear of the rose
\f1\i0\fs22  \'97 Okay?  \

\f3\i\fs24 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It won't break your nose
\f1\i0\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Although Chanta blinked his eyes and shook his head, no clarity of thought came. Not that Chanta expected clarity; he knew he wasn\'92t the brightest bulb in the amusement park. But while his mind fizzled its filament, his body reacted. He leaned in, eyes closed, pressing his nose toward where the rose floated. Chill. Tingling on his eyebrows. Then brightness behind his eyelids. He opened his eyes\'97darkness closed around his face like a feathery pillow, yielding easily as if his head passed entirely through. His vision cleared. On the other side: a wide atrium filled with greenery, like the lobby of an expensive hotel. He snapped back, and was once more standing centimetres from a solid, opalescent, black wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tried to process what he\'92d witnessed. Failed. So Chanta did what Chanta always did when faced with the inexplicable: something stupid.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f3\i\fs24 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It won\'92t break your nose
\f1\i0\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It didn\'92t. And neither was his toe stubbed, his knee ruptured, his gonads crushed, nor his ribs cracked as he, quite literally, walked into a wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And there it was again in front of him: a huge, open indoor space of opulence, of greenery and running water, and cool perfumed air. Behind him, a window with a view to the promenade and rose gardens from which he had stepped. As the shock of it subsided, fear took control of his musculoskeletal system and hurled him back through the window.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you gonna drink that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta almost jumped out of his clothes with fright. Before him, the old, greyish man pointed to the whisky bottle in his hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you gonna wear some pants?\'94 Chanta asked the still naked man.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Too late for that,\'94 the man said. \'93I need a drink. I got snakes in me boots.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You're not wearing boots.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man looked at Chanta like he\'92d caught him cheating at cards. \'93I got the jim-jams, the rattles\'85 you know.\'94 When it was clear Chanta didn\'92t know, he added, \'93I got the booze blues. I haven\'92t had a drink in two days.\'94 He licked his lips as he trained his milky eyes on the bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta hesitated; perhaps the adrenaline going sour in his system slowed him down.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Look. I'm shaking all over,\'94 the old man said, and Chanta winced as the man\'92s nether parts shook. \'93You gotta help me,\'94 the man pleaded. \'93I\'92m hallucinating.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Aren't we all?\'94 Chanta asked as he relented and handed over the bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even with shaky hands, the man swiftly knocked the stopper out of the bottle and greedily guzzled three long nips from it. Wiping his mouth with the back of his surprisingly grimy hand, he said, \'93I could swear on my mother\'92s liquor licence I saw you stick your head in that wall."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You mean like this?\'94 Chanta stuck his head in the wall. The man wailed and clutched the scotch to his bosom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You try it,\'94 Chanta instructed, after he\'92d pulled his head out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What? Are you crazy?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nah, look,\'94 Chanta said, and demonstrated. \'93It\'92s easy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man looked at Chanta and the wall, and appeared to be considering it. \'93Nah,\'94 he said at last.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, come on. Amuse me,\'94 Chanta pleaded. \'93For the grog.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man clutched the bottle tighter to his chest, assessing whether Chanta might try to seize it back. But all he saw was an innocent smile on Chanta\'92s face, a look of wonder, a sense of adventure.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh all right.\'94 The old man, figuring he should match the speed and confidence of Chanta\'92s demonstration, pushed his head towards the wall. Chanta heard a loud crack as bone collided with stone. \'93Ow!\'94 the old man exclaimed as he put his fingers tenderly to his forehead, then examined his fingertips for blood. \'93You\'92ve given me a concussion, you idiot,\'94 he said, then took a large swig of the whisky as medicine for the pain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta tangled his eyebrows together, perplexed. \'93Why would it work only for me?\'94 And to test it out, poked his arm to his elbow into the wall. \'93Try it again,\'94 he said, \'93while I\'92ve got my arm in there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man looked sceptical, but slowly pushed his hand towards the wall. His eyes bulged with surprise as his fingers, then palm, then most of his forearm slid into the wall. \'93Yikes!\'94 he said and quickly pulled out. He stared at his hand as if it had betrayed him, then at Chanta, then back at his hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There\'92s a whole seven-star hotel in there,\'94 Chanta said. \'93Wanna check it out with me?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man\'92s eyes turned greedy and he licked his lips again. \'93Hotel, you say?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It sure looks like it,\'94 Chanta said. \'93We might find you a decent set of clothes too.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, I ain\'92t proud,\'94 the man explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I can see that.\'94 Chanta grabbed the man's shoulder. \'93C\'92mon.\'94 He stepped into the wall and pulled the man with him. Into the cool, fresh air of the building's interior he stepped, revelling in the restorative sensations of greenery and perfume. Behind him, he felt the air pressure shift\'97like a door sealing shut. Then came the sound of breaking glass and a series of wet, slushing slaps as suddenly the old man\'92s shoulder fell heavily away from his guiding hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta couldn\'92t make sense of the carnage behind him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The moment Chanta passed fully through, the wall re-solidified, slicing the man through his shoulder blades in a guillotine-straight diagonal to below his navel. Chanta stared in frozen horror at the man\'92s startled face and watched an emulsion of spittle and whisky trickle from the corner of his dead, slack mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His stomach lurched. He stepped back and his heel skidded in something warm. The whisky bottle lay shattered on the polished floor between them, its contents mixing with what had spilled from the man.
